6212158203125, 0.01125335693359375, -0.0009794235229492188, -0.0014238357543945312, 0.0252685546875, -0.0014314651489257812, 0.00296783447265625, -0.00992584228515625, 0.0211029052734375, 0.0250244140625, 0.050994873046875, 0.0005359649658203125, 0.021942138671875, -0.04010009765625, -0.0242462158203125, 0.03619384765625, -0.01123046875, 0.0190582275390625, 0.006603240966796875, -0.041534423828125, -0.0146026611328125, 0.004032135009765625, -0.0026302337646484375, 0.016204833984375, 0.0231475830078125, -0.00782012939453125, -0.0018444061279296875, 0.0029964447021484375, 0.006969451904296875, 0.00777435302734375, -0.0021381378173828125, 0.006214141845703125, -0.016357421875, -0.0269012451171875, -0.0006308555603027344, -0.01371002197265625, 0.0106048583984375, -0.00124359130859375, -0.009429931640625, 0.047882080078125, -0.020111083984375, -0.01983642578125, 0.0012941360473632812, 0.0095062255859375, 0.01465606689453125, -0.04205322265625, 0.0020694732666015625, -0.0005536079406738281], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: specifically, it challenges how and where humans retain meaningful oversight and control over tasks performed by AI. In this context, the \"governance problem\" refers to the difficulty of ensuring that human direction and accountability remain visible and effective when tasks are handed off to AI systems. This article asks: at what points in the process does human direction leave observable traces when tasks are delegated to AI?\n\nWe distinguish two routes for human direction. Specified delegation places that direction before execution, through instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task\u2014such as providing a detailed prompt or explicit requirements before the AI begins. Iterative coproduction places it during production, through interventions that correct or redirect provisional outputs\u2014for example, by reviewing and revising AI-generated drafts in real time. These conceptual distinctions guide our empirical approach: we examine how observable traces of human direction, via specified delegation and iterative coproduction, manifest in different modes of AI use.\n\nTo investigate both routes, we use aggregate monthly cells\u2014summaries of user activity grouped by month and user type\u2014from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. These months were selected because they represent the most recent period with complete and validated data at the time of analysis, providing a snapshot of current usage patterns. While the findings are based on this interval, the patterns observed are likely indicative of broader trends, though future research should test their generalizability. The AEI distinguishes two modes of use: 1P API, which corresponds to direct traffic through Anthropic's application programming interface (typically used by developers or organizations integrating AI into workflows), and Claude.ai, which combines activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces (used by individuals interacting directly with the AI platform). This distinction is relevant because it reflects different user contexts and interaction patterns.\n\nOn this basis, we test whether a stronger work-related orientation of human-AI interaction is associated with more specified delegation within each mode, and whether the increase in the iterative profile is greater in Claude.ai than in 1P API. The main analysis uses level-0 O*NET tasks\u2014broad occupational task categories from the O*NET database\u2014to estimate how both profiles change when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal use to work-related use. We define node-month pairs as unique combinations of user nodes and months. For comparability and to control for confounding differences between user groups, we restrict the iterative comparison to 1,411 node-month pairs that are observed and eligible in both modes during the study period. This ensures that the same users are compared across both interaction contexts.\n\nSpecified delegation increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]). In practical terms, this means that as work-related use increases, there is a notable rise in the presence of explicit instructions or constraints, especially in the API context\u2014equivalent to a moderate shift in how tasks are framed for the AI. On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). While modest, this indicates that iterative, in-process human intervention is more responsive to work-related use in Claude.ai than in the API mode, suggesting that direct user interaction facilitates more real-time oversight.\n\nThese findings show that work-related orientation is associated with stronger traces of prior human direction and that the observable iterative response varies across modes of use. However, the study is limited by its focus on a two-month period and aggregate data, which may not capture all nuances of user behavior. Future research could extend the analysis to longer timeframes or more granular data. Overall, the article shifts attention from how much the AI executes to when human direction leaves observable traces, highlighting the importance of understanding the timing and form of human input for effective governance and oversight of AI-assisted work."}}